Here's a simple LaTeX example demonstrating an automatic table of contents (TOC):

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\begin{document}

\title{Sample Document}
\author{Author Name}
\date{\today}
\maketitle

% Generate the table of contents
\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}
This is the first section.

\subsection{Background}
A subsection example.

\section{Methodology}
Details about the methods used.

\subsection{Data Collection}
Describes how data was gathered.

\section{Conclusion}
Summary of findings.

% Compile twice for TOC to populate
\end{document}
```

Key points:
1. Use `\tableofcontents` where you want the TOC placed
2. Sectioning commands (`\section`, `\subsection`, etc.) automatically add entries
3. Compile the document twice (using pdflatex/xelatex) to resolve references
4. `\section*{}` (unnumbered sections) won't appear unless manually added with `\addcontentsline`

The TOC will automatically list sections with page numbers. For books, use `\chapter` in a `book`/`report` class. Figures/tables can be added to their own lists using `\listoffigures`/`\listoftables`.

